/-!
# Henselization and quasi-finite ring maps

Let `R → S` be a ring map, let `q` be a prime of `S` lying over the prime `p` of `R`, and assume
`R → S` is quasi-finite at `q`. Let `R_p → R_p^h` and `S_q → S_q^h` be the henselizations, and
let `R_p^h → S_q^h` be the local ring map of Lemma 04GS. Then `S_q^h` is the localization of
`R_p^h ⊗_{R_p} S_q` at the prime generated by `q`, and `R_p^h → S_q^h` is finite.

The proof: the localization `S'` of `T = R_p^h ⊗_{R_p} S_q` at the prime `Q` over `𝔪_{R_p^h}`
and `q` is a localization of the finite type `R_p^h`-algebra `R_p^h ⊗_R S`, quasi-finite at the
prime corresponding to `q` (Lemma 00PN); hence `S'` is henselian and finite over `R_p^h`
(Lemma 04GH). Being a localization of `T`, which is ind-étale over `S_q` (Lemma 0BSH), `S'` is a
filtered colimit of étale `S_q`-algebras, so the uniqueness of Lemma 08HT identifies it with
`S_q^h`.

Second, strict henselization commutes with base change along quasi-finite ring maps (Lemma 0GIP).
Let `A → B` and `A → C` be ring maps, let `D = B ⊗_A C`, let `P` be a prime of `D`, and let
`A^sh`, `B^sh`, `C^sh`, `D^sh` be strict henselizations of the localizations of `A`, `B`, `C`, `D`
at the contractions of `P`, with local ring maps forming a commutative square over
`A → B, C → D`. If `A → B` is quasi-finite at `P ∩ B`, or `B` (or `B_{P ∩ B}`) is a filtered
colimit of quasi-finite algebras, or `B` is integral over `A`, then
`B^sh ⊗_{A^sh} C^sh → D^sh` is an isomorphism.

All four cases reduce to one criterion: `B^sh` is integral over `A^sh` with purely inseparable
residue field extension, and `κ(D^sh)` is algebraic over `κ(C^sh)`. Then
`E = B^sh ⊗_{A^sh} C^sh` is local by Lemma 092Y and henselian, being integral over `C^sh`; its
residue field is `κ(D^sh)`, since `κ(D^sh)` is separable over the image of `κ(D_P)` and purely
inseparable over the separably closed `κ(C^sh)`; and the uniqueness of Lemma 08HR produces an
inverse `D^sh → E`. In case (1) the hypotheses of the criterion come from Lemma 05WR, applied to
`A → B` and, through Lemma 00PN, to `C → D`. In the other cases every finite subset of `B` comes
from a finite type algebra `S'` quasi-finite at the relevant prime; then `B^sh` is the strict
henselization of a localization `T_Q` of `A^sh ⊗_A B` (Lemma 08HV), elements of `T_Q` come from
localizations of `A^sh ⊗_A S'`, which are finite over `A^sh` by Lemma 04GH, so `T_Q` is integral
over `A^sh`, strictly henselian, and equal to `B^sh`; the same argument applies to `C → D`.

## Main results

* `IsLocalRing.IsHenselization.isLocalization_and_finite_of_quasiFiniteAt`: Stacks 05WP.
* `IsLocalRing.IsHenselization.finite_and_isPurelyInseparable_of_quasiFiniteAt`: along a
  quasi-finite ring map, the map of strict henselizations is finite with finite purely inseparable
  residue field extension (Lemma 05WR for an arbitrary strict henselization).
* `IsLocalRing.IsHenselization.isIntegral_of_forall_finset`: along a colimit of quasi-finite
  algebras, the map of strict henselizations is integral.
* `IsLocalRing.IsHenselization.bijective_tensorProduct_lift_of_quasiFiniteAt`,
  `IsLocalRing.IsHenselization.bijective_tensorProduct_lift_of_ind_quasiFinite`,
  `IsLocalRing.IsHenselization.bijective_tensorProduct_lift_of_ind_quasiFinite_localization`,
  `IsLocalRing.IsHenselization.bijective_tensorProduct_lift_of_isIntegral`: Stacks 0GIP, cases
  (1) to (4).

## Implementation notes

The localizations `R_p`, `S_q`, the henselizations `R_p^h`, `S_q^h` and the tensor product
`T = R_p^h ⊗_{R_p} S_q` are abstract: `T` is any `Algebra.IsPushout Rp Sq Rh T`. The map
`R_p^h → S_q^h` of Lemma 04GS is the algebra structure `algebraMap Rh Sh`, required local and
compatible with `R_p → S_q → S_q^h`; by Lemma 04GS these two conditions determine it. The map
`T → S_q^h` is the algebra structure `algebraMap T Sh`, compatible with `R_p^h` and `S_q`; by the
universal property of the pushout it is the map induced by the square. The prime "generated by
`q`" is the ideal `𝔪_{S_q} T` generated by the image of `q S_q`; that it is prime (indeed it is
the inverse image of `𝔪_{S_q^h}`) is part of the conclusion. As in Lemma 04GH, quasi-finiteness
at `q` in the sense of the source is `Algebra.FiniteType R S` together with
`Algebra.QuasiFiniteAt R q`.

In Lemma 0GIP the separably algebraically closed field `κ` with `D → κ` enters only through the
prime `P = ker(D → κ)` and the maps of Lemma 04GU attached to the embeddings of the residue fields
of the strict henselizations in `κ`. The statement here is proved for an arbitrary prime `P` and
arbitrary local ring maps between arbitrary strict henselizations forming a commutative square over
`A → B, C → D`; this includes the square of the source. A strict henselization of `X_𝔭` is
`IsHenselization X_𝔭 κ(X^sh) X^sh` with `κ(X^sh)` a separable algebraic closure of `κ(𝔭)`, as in
Lemma 08HV. The map `B^sh ⊗_{A^sh} C^sh → D^sh` is `Algebra.TensorProduct.lift` of the two maps
into `D^sh`, and the isomorphism is its bijectivity. A filtered colimit of quasi-finite algebras is
`ObjectProperty.ind` in the category of algebras of the property of being of finite type and
quasi-finite; case (4) is not reduced to case (2) through a presentation of `B` as a colimit of
finite algebras, but through its finite subalgebras directly.

The proof of case (1) departs from the source in its last step. The source identifies both
`B^sh ⊗_{A^sh} C^sh` and `D^sh` with localizations of `B ⊗_A C^sh` and concludes from locality of
the former; here, once `B^sh ⊗_{A^sh} C^sh` is known to be local (Lemma 092Y, as in the source),
its residue field is identified with `κ(D^sh)` and an inverse is produced by Lemma 08HR. The proof
of cases (2) to (4) does not pass through `B^sh = colim B_i^sh`; it shows directly that `B^sh` is
integral over `A^sh`, which with the criterion above is what the source's reduction to case (1)
provides.

